ne Robotics Customer Support (Tier 2), 25 September (vendor-account): 'We've reviewed your ticket. Firmware 6.4 has been live at three other customer sites since July with no change in protective-stop rates, so we don't see the firmware as the cause. Increases like this are usually environmental: people, layout, clutter. If the stops are hurting throughput, a common remedy is to shorten the protective field by 20%, and we can push that profile to your fleet remotely. Our next release, 6.4.2, is planned for early December and includes further filtering refinements.'\n- Interview notes, R. Szabo, robot maintenance technician, 12 October (staff-account): 'Most of the stops I get called out to are in the marshalling lanes, A and B, where outbound pallets are staged. There are hardly any in C, the tote zone. Usually by the time I get there the field's clear and nobody's around, but people walk off, so I can't say who it was. Somebody asked me about the new shiny pallet wrap; procurement switched suppliers at the start of August. I don't buy it. We had that wrap for a month before the stops went up and nothing changed. If it was the wrap, August would have been bad too.'\n- Outbound performance dashboard, weeks 30–41 (system-record): Missed carrier cut-offs averaged 2 per week in weeks 30–35 and 9 per week in weeks 37–41. Outbound pallet volume has been up 18% since week 35 as the seasonal peak ramped. Comparison row for the same weeks in 2025: cut-off misses rose from 2 to 7 per week during that peak, while collision stops stayed near 200 per week. Dashboard note: robot stop time is not linked to wave completion data.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe stop increase comes from an interaction between two changes. The new firmware (6.4) now treats faint short-range reflections as obstacles, and the glossy stretch film on outbound pallets produces those reflections at oblique angles. Neither change causes the problem alone.\n\nEvidence: upgraded pallet robots in Zones A/B went from 3.2 to 15.6 stops per 100 robot-hours. Robots still on 6.3 in the same zones stayed flat at about 3.2. Tote robots on 6.4 in Zone C, which has no wrapped pallets, were also flat. The bench test with no people present gave 17 of 20 stops for 6.4 with gloss film. It gave 0 or 1 of 20 for every other combination. About 93% of the added stops have no person's badge within 3 m, so the temps are not the driver.\n\nWhat to do: switch outbound pallets back to the previous matte film, keep firmware as is, and track stops by zone and firmware group. Don't adopt the three-strike policy or the 20% shorter protective fields. Don't rely on Halvane's December release until it is tested against the gloss film. Rolling back to 6.3 is a fallback only. Security support for 6.3 ends 31 October.\n\nConfidence is high on the mechanism. It is lower on how much the stops contribute to the missed cut-offs, which also track the 18% volume increase.\n","numLines":26,"startLine":1,"totalLines":26}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01USnq58TqHFrLzru1zPHEWQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
